    <h2>How it all started</h2>
    <hr class="rule">
    <p>I never thought I'd be the «relationships» person. I was the events person — photoshoots, organising other people's celebrations, giving people a beautiful day. Meanwhile my own was coming apart at the seams.</p>
    <p>We'd been together 8 years. And at some point I landed in exactly the spot I now talk to women about: you love him — and living together is unbearable. And from every direction, the same thing — «just leave him, you deserve better», «why do you even need this», «walk away».</p>
    <p>I was told to leave — so I left. It was one of the most thoughtless decisions of my life.</p>

    <h2>Why I'm into this at all</h2>
    <hr class="rule">
    <p>I'd already been through one marriage that ended — and I really didn't want to walk that road a second time. My mother lived with my father until his death. My grandmothers and great-grandmothers on both sides — the same story: there were no break-ups «just because», ever. If a couple ended, it ended only with the death of one of the two, sooner or later. Not with divorce, not with «we just drifted apart».</p>
    <p>Coming back wasn't easy. So much so that I went not to a psychologist — but to a psychiatrist with thirty years of experience. Because what was happening inside me needed exactly that level of help, and I admitted that honestly.</p>
    <p>And that's when I started to really work it out — what happens between people, why relationships fall apart, and whether it could have gone differently. I read Gottman, studied attachment theory, took apart the mechanics of what had happened, piece by piece. I talked a lot with my mother, trying to understand in detail the secret of her marriage.</p>
    <p>I applied all of it. And the relationship that was falling apart at the seams became the one I'm in now — 11 years already. That's when I understood I wanted to show this to others.</p>

    <h2>My approach</h2>
    <hr class="rule">
    <p>I'm <strong>for family</strong>. Not out of morality, but because I've seen how often «freedom» turns into loneliness, and «he'll never change» into not wanting to change yourself.</p>
    <p>But I'm not for «put up with it at any cost» either. If something can be repaired — we repair it. Honestly, without rose-tinted sentiment and without blame. I don't teach you to endure, and I don't teach you to leave. I teach you to stop walking the same circle.</p>

    <h2>What I build on</h2>
    <hr class="rule">
    <p>Not esoterics and not «feminine energy». On what's been tested for decades: John Gottman's research (from the way a couple talks, he predicted divorce with close to 90% accuracy) and Sue Johnson's emotionally focused therapy. Plus attachment theory — the very one the free test starts with.</p>
    <p>I simply translated it all from the scientific language into the human one. And added a little irony — there's no doing without it.</p>
    </div><!-- /aboutBlocks -->

